#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060901 :tutorial/content "All the angles we have constructed so far have been inside the range ![](images/fa/fa8919b9a8968214967b7c4b868b1eb64a95c1d0d2068bd312a4a4bb11ad4e6f.svg) However, we can also construct angles outside this range.\n\nThe way we do this involves **coterminal angles**. Coterminal angles are different angles that appear as the same angle in the coordinate plane. For example, the angles ![](images/d2/d22511f2212b05e9408f52f8da19a125897737f033bb64f480780f2f024b2091.svg) and ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) are coterminal because a rotation of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/d5/d5c42dddda6dd2e3707f543621c5ab3ef3557e26aa8e938ffddc686917238fdd.svg) In general, whenever we add or subtract a multiple of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) we get a coterminal angle.\n\nThe standard way in which we draw a **positive** angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) in the coordinate plane is as follows:\n\n- Subtract integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to represent the angle ![](images/fa/fae5f1a011f55464bbc26a61e49aa6c4b2e2b9fb2076370a05f35c00de3a7a29.svg) in the coordinate plane, we first subtract ![](images/29/2903c04e0275e76d718eff7ae8277ecb8b5404777225212dca2507b93907ab41.svg)\n\n![](images/3b/3b3bf36f7e1ccf241036659c484ffa0dd17884f8044f6bd6faf6240764552c41.svg)\n\nThe result, ![](images/73/73c6bd81124f4f9c92e0d8ed37449d8dd5be227f9e4b05b2f7953d28ddff7d6c.svg) is a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg) So, to draw the angle ![](images/63/637324a8ad2f6d52227a2fe46d938a816aab3de3d50e0f9ee035f602537e2091.svg) we draw the coterminal angle ![](images/33/33cc1d85e29c1ae4ce3b019b0e49a5dba77efefa6f405590a91fce42f504baf3.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/bb/bbeb3dfdcb56e4f7f1a39010dc798af004f3ab5ee843f653e86f80fe9974dd30.png)\n\n**Note:** For every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) we subtract, we make one complete rotation counter-clockwise around the origin. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle.\n\nHere, we subtracted ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) a single time, so we make a single rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac16cf651b6f5e16d3f31418646388dac16384c4824e0095bf9a925c13b1ea77.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c50e7f7-6a06-51b1-8970-2f34f25890c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060902 :tutorial/content "To find a coterminal angle of a given angle ![](images/1f/1f216b45120e801bbdcf2c3f165ac341a7dd0bd305f87381647b205831b06725.svg) we rotate ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) clockwise or counter-clockwise from ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) We could do this again and again, each time looping by ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) to find an infinite number of coterminal angles:\n![](images/bc/bcd7ec5620afdf6f142437b0a0aedcddcee4678309d9d2328da3892eb0034730.svg)\n\nFor instance, the angles ![](images/ed/ed05bb3b845471f7526114d38f3c1a525aeb653d50a50b3ebe0457266e275d5f.svg) ![](images/94/9476642d5d3f3e418c71f7b062f33f894fa648e3c0cee6a15fc359b43889c19a.svg) ![](images/94/94f4422d3745b4401e7c9517c9e08382bb4d4af59a4f9ef164500530b530a525.svg) and ![](images/98/98e07f13eb10e759924519f7b068aa9f9768dcd7c362088b89f1831c886a3a2d.svg) are all coterminal.\n\n  \n\n![](images/c2/c2949532fb08722c3dbaaad3cd971ae2c3d3e7d7606f051da6dccd2bc3cfecc6.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a87549c4-ee2e-552b-95ea-50727cc87fbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2710 :tutorial/title "Finding Multiple Coterminal Angles"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060903 :tutorial/content "The method for constructing a *negative* angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) outside the range ![](images/3b/3bbae843070f7afe0fc1c253cc8dd663ed4571cc5d250bebae728dd3298cee8a.svg) is similar to the method for positive angles, but with one main difference: we *add* multiples of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) instead of subtracting. So the steps are:\n\n- Add integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angle ![](images/78/78826f360e99c31e2e800e0691e6770af9a57442c1b09212bb94f3df8a7a7e6c.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bcc5c8dedaeca80ac8c48f4d5d18aa3666e87b5be942f1a49d187f99ade28435.svg)\n\nNow, we draw the angle ![](images/20/20615d4147fe63007acc775caaa7d175edca7e0717a9e1224b4a66d490df2316.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/78/785973780f0ed538c0a0f430a402c25fb562a7308911f1d5cecc958da02b99f2.png)\n\nAgain, for every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) that we add, we make one complete rotation around the origin clockwise. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle." :tutorial/id #uuid "b9973405-ead1-5dfa-92ff-238e697e4ad2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2712 :tutorial/title "Constructing Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}]}
